frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Marimba A4 over La Favre's A4 tube, its stop set to sit the tube on this bar; its loss Hill's printed whole-bar loss factor in place of the three-bar fit; everything else as the A4 tube card.
name	marimba-a4-hill-tubetuned-rosewood-rubber
source	Bar outline: J. La Favre, lafavre.us/marimba-bars.htm, table of an unnamed commercial 5-octave marimba with rosewood bars: A row, length 12 15/16 in, width 1 7/8 in, thickness 0.93 in. Tube: the same page, La Favre's own padouk marimba, A4 resonator: aluminium tubing, outer diameter 2 1/4 in, wall 1/16 in, air column 6 3/4 in at 72 F, straight, 11/16 in below the bottom of the bar. Wood: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544, Rosewood bar: E 2.13e10, density 1015, eta 2.35e-7 s, gamma_B 51.58 1/s, through the packs named below (E and density are read from the pack of Doutaut and Chaigne 1994, which prints the same values). Ratios: USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282, Honduras mahogany, stand-in. Mallet: the same Table I, Korogi No. 4 rubber, K 3.7e7, 23.6 g, measured on a padouk bar; 1.0 m/s and strike 15.9 cm of 29.3 cm as the paper's Fig. 8.
note	NOT in the source: the tube's stop. La Favre prints an air column of 6 3/4 in (171.450 mm) at A4 = 442, and that column sits 52.1 c sharp of this bar, so this card sets the stop as a maker would, at 177.197 mm (5.75 mm further down), solved once on the passive column with the bar's reflection in it so its first resonance sits on this bar's f1 of 440.000 Hz; the tube's cross-section and gap are as printed and no recording enters. La Favre set each stopper by ear and says the figures aren't absolute. La Favre's total length of 9 9/16 in leaves 2 13/16 in below the printed stop, for the stopper assembly and tuning up to 85 F; the assembly's thickness isn't printed, so whether this stop can reach isn't known. NOT in the source either: the undercut (solved here in the engine on this card's 56 x 8 x 5 mesh, modes only, a circular arc, half reach 63.161 mm, middle 9.721 mm, so f1 sits at equal temperament and f2 = 4 f1, from the builder's statement for its own padouk bars on the same page; the rosewood table prints no arch length and no tuning). Strike fraction carried from the xylophone bar; no marimba strike is printed. Not the recording's bar or mallet: Iowa states no bar wood and no bar outline; the recording's file names a cord mallet or a roll, and a Korogi rubber xylophone mallet is not that mallet. Tube and bar come from two instruments: the bar from the unnamed commercial table, the tube from La Favre's own marimba, printed at A4 = 442 and 72 F, rendered here at A4 = 440 in the card's gas; no correction made. "Bottom of bar" is read as the plane of the uncut lower face, so the wood at the bar's middle is a further 13.9 mm from the mouth. The tube is a closed-open column of three odd modes, every one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. Its mouth, 54.0 mm across inside, is wider than this 47.6 mm bar. The loss is one printed whole-bar loss factor, eta 0.0045 at every mode, the mean as printed (the printed range, 0.0036 to 0.0068, is its limit), from H. Hill's unrefereed 2011 student report (NSF REU, University of Illinois Physics 406): the bars of a Yamaha YM-5100A, Honduras rosewood per the maker's spec for that model, recorded in a room with the resonators off, so eta holds the wood, the cord and the bar's own radiation, and this card's tube adds only its own losses. The stiffness and density stay the rosewood rows cited above, so the loss and the stiffness come from different rosewoods. It was tried after seeing that the pages decay too fast on the three-bar fit, with no recording number in it.
